messages:
Olivia: Hey infra, ran `terraform plan` in staging after we swapped the default StorageClass from gp2 -> gp3 for new clusters. Plan is showing PVCs (and underlying PV/ebs) will be replaced for existing apps — ~5 PVCs for one service. We didn't touch the existing PVs manually. Anyone seen terraform propose replaces just because storageClass changed?

Ramon: can you paste the plan diff or the exact resource line that's changing?

Olivia: Here is the snippet from plan (trimmed):
```
# kubernetes_persistent_volume_claim.app_db[0] will be replaced
~ resource "kubernetes_persistent_volume_claim" "app_db" {
    spec {
-     storage_class_name = "gp2"
+     storage_class_name = "gp3"
      access_modes       = ["ReadWriteOnce"]
      resources {
        requests = {
          storage = "50Gi"
        }
      }
    }
}
```
It looks like TF thinks storage_class_name changed which forces replacement.

Harper: PVC.storageClassName is immutable in Kubernetes once bound. If the PV is already provisioned with gp2, the PVC will be recreated by k8s/Terraform unless the PV itself is updated. Did you create these PVCs originally with gp2?

Natalie: check the actual PV object for one of them: `kubectl get pv pv-0a1b2c -o yaml` — I bet spec.storageClassName is gp2. You can patch PV to gp3: `kubectl patch pv pv-0a1b2c -p '{"spec":{"storageClassName":"gp3"}}'` and then plan should not show replacement, but patching PVs is risky and might break the provisioner.

Ramon: Another safer option: keep the PVCs as-is but tell Terraform to ignore changes to storage_class_name so plan doesn't try to replace. Example in TF k8s resource: 
```hcl
resource "kubernetes_persistent_volume_claim" "app_db" {
  ...
  lifecycle {
    ignore_changes = [spec[0].storage_class_name]
  }
}
```
That makes plan stable while you sort an actual migration.

Olivia: Good point. We're using the HashiCorp k8s provider 2.x, so the attribute path should be `spec.0.storage_class_name` in HCL. Will test on a single canary PVC.

Harper: FYI if the PV is dynamic and the provisioner expects gp2/gp3 semantics (IOPS/capabilities), patching the PV may not be recommended. If you want to migrate volumes to gp3 properly, you'll need to snapshot & restore to new PVCs (ensuring app downtime) or use your cloud provider's volume migration tooling.

Natalie: Also double-check whether the PVCs are managed by a separate terraform module / state. If PVs/PVCs live outside the same state, TF diff will still show recreation because the provider only sees the declared desired state.

devops-bot: Reminder: ignoring changes will silence drift alerts. If you add lifecycle ignore_changes, open a ticket to track the storageclass migration plan so we don't silently drift.

Olivia: Thanks all — I'll add the lifecycle ignore_changes to the module for a canary (single replica), run plan, and report back. If that looks good we'll escalate a migration runbook.

Ramon: ping here when you have the canary plan results.

Olivia: Will do — thanks :thumbsup:
